WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.537
DALE MARKOWITZ: So here on
"Making With Machine Learning,"

00:00:02.537 --> 00:00:03.960
I record all my
videos in English,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.270
because it's the only
language that I speak.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.020
But you guys?

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.490
You're from all over
the world and you speak

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.720
a lot of different languages.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.580
Right now, we do
translate it as subs,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:15.180
but I wondered if we could
use AI to translate it dubs.

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.430
[NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]

00:00:19.352 --> 00:00:21.810
DALE MARKOWITZ: I got this idea
from my extremely inspiring

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.270
co-worker here at
Google named Markku.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.490
A couple of months ago,
Markku filmed a YouTube video

00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:29.460
showing you how you could create
real-time translated subtitles

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:31.560
that would actually be
projected on your body,

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:33.640
and even helped me
build my own version.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.560
So I thought, OK.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.240
He did speech to
text, but why not

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.405
add AI text-to-speech
like an AI voice actor?

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.280
Now, I know a lot of
you said in the comments

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:42.833
you hate computer voices.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:44.500
SPEAKER 1: But I don't
mind them at all.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.710
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
here's how I did it.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.180
This project is actually
kind of straightforward.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:51.030
First, I'll pull the
audio out of my videos

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.530
and then I'll use
the Speech-to-Text

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.180
API to transcribe them.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:55.890
Next, I'll use the
Translation API

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:58.430
to convert the transcripts
to any language that I want.

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:01.140
And then finally, I'll use the
Text-to-Speech API to speak

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.615
those translated transcripts.

00:01:03.615 --> 00:01:06.690
OK, so the first step is to
enable some Google Cloud APIs.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.870
For this project, we'll
need the Speech-to-Text API,

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.840
the Text-to-Speech API,
and the Translation API.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.773
I decided to do this
project in Python,

00:01:14.773 --> 00:01:17.190
and you can find all of my
code in the Making with Machine

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.750
Learning GitHub repo.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:22.020
First, I use a library
called pydub to extract

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.530
the audio from the video
file and save it as a wav.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:28.290
Next, I temporarily uploaded
that file to the cloud

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.870
so that it could be used
with the Speech to Text API.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.540
Here's where I actually call
the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:34.200
Client.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:35.700
There are a couple
of different ways

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.470
that you can configure this API.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.060
First, you specify
the language_code,

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.160
which is what language
you're transcribing from.

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.170
enable_automatic_punctuation
tells

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:44.820
the API to look out
for punctuation,

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:46.530
like question marks or periods.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.930
enable_word_time_offsets
gives you

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.820
both the word and the
exact time the speaker

00:01:50.820 --> 00:01:53.550
said them. diarization_config
tells the API to look out

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:54.810
for multiple speakers.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:57.870
And you also have the ability
to use enhanced, more accurate

00:01:57.870 --> 00:01:59.658
models for certain languages.

00:01:59.658 --> 00:02:01.200
By the way, here's
an example of what

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.807
the data that actually comes
out of that API looks like.

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:05.640
Next, we have to chunk
the transcript output

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.400
so that we can feed it
to the Translation API.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.930
At first, I thought
I could do this

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.630
by splitting words into
sentences based on punctuation

00:02:12.630 --> 00:02:15.670
mark, and then translating
each individual sentence.

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:17.820
But then, like a dummy,
I realized that that only

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:19.170
works for Romance languages.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:21.820
And in Japanese, you can't
just split sentences by period.

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.700
So instead, I had
to check for gaps

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.868
in when the speaker started
and stopped speaking.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:28.410
And then those were
the chunks that I

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.810
passed to the Translation API.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.150
Actually calling the
Translation API only takes

00:02:33.150 --> 00:02:34.470
a couple of lines of code.

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.870
Here's what the results
of the Translation API

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:39.910
look like on my transcripts.

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.970
Finally, I called the
Text-to-Speech API

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.770
to speak the translated words.

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:46.800
You can configure the API to
use different computer voices,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.690
and you can control the
speaking rate, which

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.970
I used to make sure that
the computer voice sped

00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:52.680
up or slowed down
to match the rate

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:54.060
of the speaker in the video.

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:55.950
SPEAKER 1: Sometimes,
I talk very fast.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:57.920
And sometimes, I talk more slow.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.210
DALE MARKOWITZ: OK, so it's
about midnight right now,

00:03:00.210 --> 00:03:02.850
but I just got all of the
individual pieces of the dubber

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:03.430
working.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.985
So I'm going to try it
off for the first time.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:11.280
MARKKU: [SPEAKING FINNISH]

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:21.220
SPEAKER 2: Welcome to the
no time testing center shed.

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:23.560
We are here collecting
learning and various sports

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.880
using this picture scooter.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.535
So send the helicopter into the
air and start collecting data.

00:03:27.535 --> 00:03:29.410
DALE MARKOWITZ: There
are actually two places

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:30.670
where errors could come from.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:33.087
First of all, the Speech-to-Text
API could make a mistake.

00:03:33.087 --> 00:03:35.320
It could mishear me
and mistranscibe me.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.925
But also, translation
can be inaccurate.

00:03:37.925 --> 00:03:39.550
So I'm going to show
you two approaches

00:03:39.550 --> 00:03:41.050
to upping the accuracy.

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.900
First, we can up the quality
of Speech-to-Text by using

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.730
a feature called phraseHints.

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:47.350
Here, you can specify
words or phrases

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.600
that you think are more
likely to appear in the video,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.190
and then the API will be more
likely to recognize them,

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.850
especially if they're uncommon
words or proper nouns.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:56.740
For translation, we
can use something

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:59.050
called a glossary,
which is just a CSV that

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.540
specifies the way we want
certain words or phrases to be

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.650
translated.

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:04.930
For example, in Markku's
video, the translations

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.280
for drone and tarp
were a little off.

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:09.370
So even though Markku
said tarp in Finnish,

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.560
the API was
translating it to shed.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:14.175
Using a glossary, we can
make spot fixes like this.

00:04:14.175 --> 00:04:15.550
Putting these two
tools together,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:18.290
I was able to make the accuracy
of the dubs a lot better.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:21.700
They're not perfect, but
here's what they sound like.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.370
SPEAKER 2: Welcome to the AI
coach testing center tarp.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:27.550
We are here to collect learning
data on various sports using

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:28.570
this image scooter.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.552
So send the drone into the
air and start collecting data.

00:04:31.552 --> 00:04:33.760
DALE MARKOWITZ: Let's listen
to a few other examples.

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.600
MARKKU: [SPEAKING FINNISH]

00:04:37.478 --> 00:04:39.520
SPEAKER 2: I have to make
a New Year's resolution

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:41.660
to eat less treats
and play more sports.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.940
SPEAKER 3: [SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:04:45.538 --> 00:04:47.080
SPEAKER 4: There
have been companies,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:49.432
non-profit organizations,
and educational institutes.

00:04:49.432 --> 00:04:50.890
DALE MARKOWITZ: So
I always thought

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:52.807
it would make sense to
take something simpler,

00:04:52.807 --> 00:04:54.490
like software engineering.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:57.310
SPEAKER 5: [SPEAKING RUSSIAN]

00:04:57.633 --> 00:04:59.050
DALE MARKOWITZ:
What do you think?

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.030
Would you watch one of
these AI-dubbed videos?

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:02.447
Let me know in the
comments below.

00:05:02.447 --> 00:05:04.500
And I'll see you next time.

